THE PEAK, DECOMPOSED — massif instead of heaptrack, and the decomposition SUMS.

date:   2026-09-03
host:   r7900x — AMD Ryzen 7900X, 24 threads, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, 29 GiB.
        NOT the aarch64 box the RSS records use. HEAP, not RSS.
commit: 01ef6f1f1 (main) — the tree this measures. A `--release` build with
        `CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2` added for the file:line half; the peak
        it reports is the same as the plain release build's to within massif's
        own resolution (138.39 M vs heaptrack's 139.62 M on the same cell, the
        difference being massif's accounting of its own bookkeeping).
tool:   valgrind 3.26 `--tool=massif --time-unit=B --detailed-freq=4
        --max-snapshots=300 --threshold=0.05`, peak snapshot read directly.
cell:   gradient 2048x2048 (4 MP) qp 40 preset 13, 8-bit 4:2:0, ONE encode per
        arm, arms as in benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta.
        Every arm produced its `.obu` (70,893 / 139,700 / 139,762 B).

=============== WHY MASSIF AND NOT MORE HEAPTRACK ==============================

`mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta` says of its own site table: "**A LEAD LIST, NOT
A DECOMPOSITION** — heaptrack_print says so itself for merged backtraces ('the
merged peak consumption is not correct'): these are per-site peaks that do NOT
co-occur and do NOT sum." That is the correct reading of that tool's output and
it is why the lead list could not be turned into a budget.

massif answers the other question. Its **peak snapshot** is one instant — the
allocation tree of what is LIVE SIMULTANEOUSLY at the high-water mark — so its
entries DO sum to the peak, and "what is live across a frame" is readable off
it directly. Both tools were pointed at the same cell and agree on the peak
(138.39 vs 139.62 M); they disagree only about what a "site total" means.

=============== 1. THE PEAK IS A RAMP THAT ENDS WHERE MD ENDS =================

massif's time series (191 snapshots, inter arm) is not a plateau. It is a
MONOTONIC RISE through the superblock walk, a peak at the END of the walk, and
a cliff:

  frame 1:  63.1 -> 136.6 M over snapshots 4..80, then drops to 65.9
  frame 2:  63.5 -> 137.3 M over snapshots 88..176, peak 138.39, then 51.9

The videokey arm has the same single ramp (~50 -> 95.61, then 38.8). **Peak
heap is a linear function of how many superblocks have been DECIDED but not yet
entropy-coded**, on every arm — which is the lifetime property
`mem_heaptrack_satd_2026-09-03.meta` named, now seen directly instead of
inferred.

=============== 2. WHAT IS LIVE AT EACH ARM'S PEAK, IN MB =====================

Attributed to the first non-`alloc::`/`core::` frame above each allocation, so
the column sums to the arm's peak.

  site                                         still    vkey   inter
  perf_encode::main                 (HARNESS)  12.58   18.87   25.17
  perf_encode::translate            (HARNESS)   0.00    0.00    6.29
  leaf_funnel::tx_pipeline::tx_unit_inner      26.74   27.26   10.09
  partition::funnel_block_decision              0.52    0.00   16.14
  pipeline::encode_tile_rows::{closure#0}       0.00    0.00   16.02
  picture::DecodedPictureBuffer::refresh       13.45   13.45   13.45
  inter_me::context::MeB64Output::reset         0.00    0.00   12.53
  inter_me_arm::PaPlane::refill_from_plane      0.00    4.77    9.54
  pipeline::EncodePipeline
      ::encode_frame_impl::{closure#9}          0.00    0.00    7.15
  picture::PaddedPlane::from_plane              7.15    7.15    0.00
  partition::encode_fixed_tree                  2.14    4.39    0.00
  pipeline::gather_rows                         4.19    4.19    4.19
  <u8 as SpecFromElem>::from_elem
      <- encode_frame_impl                      4.19    4.19    4.19
  pipeline::EncodePipeline::encode_frame_impl   2.46    2.53    4.19
  inter_me_arm::PaPlane::refill_decimate        0.00    1.48    2.96
  deblock::DeblockGeom::new                     2.62    2.62    2.62
  encode_frame_impl::{closure#53}               2.10    2.10    0.00
  encode_frame_impl::{closure#2}                2.10    2.10    2.10
  everything below 0.5 M                        0.48    0.51    1.76
  ------------------------------------------- ------  ------  ------
  ARM PEAK                                     80.72   95.61  138.39

The last row is a CHECK, not a rounding: the per-site column sums to the arm's
peak to the byte, because a massif peak snapshot is one instant. That is the
property `mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta`'s lead list does not have.

Two entries need naming because they are the SAME BYTES seen from different
call paths, and reading them as separate structures is a mistake:

  * `tx_unit_inner` and `funnel_block_decision` are both the frame's retained
    QUANTIZED COEFFICIENTS. `funnel_block_decision` either `mem::take`s the
    packed txb the transform pipeline allocated (site = `tx_unit_inner`) or, on
    a block with a 64-dim side, allocates the full raster itself (site =
    `funnel_block_decision`). Which site a byte lands under is decided by the
    block shape, not by a difference in what is stored. Their SUM is the
    quantity: 27.26 M videokey, 26.23 M inter, 27.26 M still.
  * `PaddedPlane::from_plane` (still/videokey) and
    `encode_frame_impl::{closure#9}` (inter) are both 7.15 M and swap places
    between the arms.

=============== 3. THE HARNESS IS 19-31 MB OF IT, NOT 6.29 ====================

`mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta` accounted for the harness as
`perf_encode::translate` = 6.29 M, "the SAME 6.29 MB sits on the port's side,
so the harness growth CANCELS in the arm difference". **That undercounts it by
one whole frame.** `perf_encode` holds THREE copies of the sequence:

  y / u / v   the generated source planes            6.29 M
  yuv         the concatenation written to `.yuv`    6.29 M per frame
  frames      one owned (fy, fu, fv) per frame       6.29 M per frame

so `perf_encode::main` is 12.58 M on the 1-frame still arm, 18.87 M on the
1-frame videokey arm (which also builds `frames`), and 25.17 M on the 2-frame
inter arm, with `translate` carrying frame 1's copy on top. Totals: still
12.58, videokey 18.87, inter **31.45** M. C's `perf_c_encode` holds ONE copy
(`main` = 6.29 M / 12.58 M for 1 / 2 frames).

  ENCODER-SIDE PEAK, both harnesses subtracted, 2048x2048:
    arm        port      C        port/C
    still      68.14 M   109.43 M  0.62x
    videokey   76.74 M   107.53 M  0.71x
    inter     106.94 M   107.54 M  0.99x

The videokey->inter marginal cost, encoder-side, is port +30.2 M against C's
+0.01 M — not the +37.65 M `mem_heaptrack_satd_2026-09-03.meta` records, which
subtracted only `translate`.

=============== 4. THE COEFFICIENT STORE IS AT PARITY WITH C, NOT EXCESS ======

The port retains ~26-27 M of quantized coefficients per frame at 4 MP (§2), and
the obvious reading — "the port holds the whole frame's coefficients where C
packs a superblock and releases" — is WRONG. C does exactly the same thing:
`svt_aom_me_sb_results_ctor`'s sibling in `pcs.c:348-368` allocates
`quantized_coeff`, one `EbPictureBufferDesc` per b64, `bit_depth =
EB_THIRTYTWO_BIT`, `PICTURE_BUFFER_DESC_FULL_MASK` (Y+U+V), `sb_size x
sb_size` — i.e. 4 bytes per luma pixel plus 4 per chroma sample, for the whole
frame, allocated once at PCS construction. At 4 MP that is 25.2 MB, and it is
`svt_aom_pic_buf_desc_pool_ctor` = **26.18 M** in
`benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.txt`'s C column, measured.

So the port's per-block `Vec<i32>` retention reproduces C's shape at C's size.
The difference is only WHEN: C allocates the pool once and reuses it every
frame, the port builds it per frame — which is why C's marginal inter-frame
cost is ~0 and the port's is not, and why the port is nonetheless LIGHTER in
absolute terms on every arm.

=============== NOT ESTABLISHED ===============================================
  * Any RSS number. This is heap. See benchmarks/mem_mecand_2026-09-03.tsv for
    the RSS half of the same grid.
  * Any aarch64 number. Different allocator, different page size.
  * Any size but 2048x2048, any preset but 13, content but gradient, qp but 40.
  * Repeat variance: one encode per arm.
  * That massif's absolute peak equals heaptrack's — it does not (138.39 vs
    139.62 M). Compare DECOMPOSITIONS from massif and TOTALS from heaptrack.
